</figure>
<div id="Sx4.SSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a limited parameter budget, which types of rank settings yield the best performance?
To answer the above question, we conduct experiments on four tasks of GLUE benchmark, limiting the total number of trainable parameters to 0.79M on RoBERTa-large.
For simplicity and parameter-efficiency considerations, we only apply low-rank adaptation to the attention weights and keep the FFN modules frozen.
This parameter budget is equivalent to <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=8</annotation></semantics></math> if the low-rank adaptation is applied to two types of weight matrices and <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=16</annotation></semantics></math> when applied to one type.
When adapting to the <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> weights, we employed the asymmetric initialization method of AILoRA.
For other weight types, the default initialization method of LoRA is used.
The experimental results are summarized in Table <a href="#Sx4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Experiments on Weight Matrix Selection ‣ Experiments ‣ AILoRA: Function-Aware Asymmetric Initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Notably, allocating the entire parameter budget to the <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> weights almost results in the poorest performance, as previously highlighted in LoRA.
Moreover, applying low-rank adaptation to more than one type of weight matrices generally leads to better performance.
At last, applying AILoRA to the <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.SSx7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> weights yields the best results, demonstrating the effectiveness of our weight matrices selection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate the distinct functional roles of the <math id="Sx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> projection matrices in the self-attention mechanism and present <span id="Sx5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AILoRA</span>, a novel parameter-efficient fine-tuning method inspired by these insights.
AILoRA introduces a function-aware asymmetric initialization scheme, leveraging the principal components of <math id="Sx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the minor components of <math id="Sx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to initialize the respective LoRA modules.
This design enables LoRA to better exploit the functional asymmetry of attention parameters, leading to improved downstream performance and faster convergence.
Extensive experiments across diverse model architectures (encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder), parameter scales (ranging from 184M to 7B), and downstream tasks (including both NLU and NLG benchmarks) consistently validate the effectiveness and robustness of AILoRA.</p>
</div>
</section>
